          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Traveling Waves on Transmission Lines, Reflection, and Bewley Lattice Diagrams",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Network overvoltages and insulation coordination\n\n## Engineering overview\n\nA high-voltage installation is an electromagnetic network whose insulation is exposed to operating-frequency voltage, temporary overvoltage, slow-front switching transients and fast-front lightning transients. A single nominal-voltage label cannot summarize this duty. This unit follows source events through reflections, equipment stress and protective choice without treating an abstract margin as a standard rating.\n\nFour separate studies are developed in this unit: **Stress categories and event time scales**, **Surge impedance and boundary reflections**, **Temporary overvoltage and resonance**, and **Coordination margins and withstand evidence**. Their models have intentionally different input assumptions. They should not be combined simply because their numerical outputs share units. A source voltage, local electric field, risk probability and event count answer different questions and can have different uncertainty structures.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of the unit, a reader should be able to state the physical source of the phenomenon; calculate a constrained model result with consistent units; identify its boundary conditions and initial data; conduct a controlled sensitivity test; and construct a short engineering review that explicitly identifies omitted mechanisms. The reader should also be able to separate a documented equipment rating from a quantity invented for an instructional example. A defensible result includes both a number and a statement of its limitations.\n\n## Evidence workflow\n\n1. **Source:** identify what event or operating condition creates the stress or observation. Write down its time reference and appropriate signal representation.\n2. **Propagation or internal response:** trace the signal through the relevant network, insulating material or sensor. Use the model only where its assumptions apply.\n3. **Measurement:** name the quantities that would have to be observed for a real analysis, noting uncertainty, calibration and reference location.\n4. **Decision:** state an actionable conclusion only when the acceptance criterion, equipment definition and hazard boundary are independently documented.\n\n## Integrated software case\n\nModel a three-node ideal transmission network in software. Separate the first wavefront from subsequent reflections and report which observation would be required before the simulated voltage is associated with physical equipment. Start with the default inputs and write a small table of three cases: baseline, one input increased, and a contrasting configuration. Give units for every column. Do not treat a \u201cbetter-looking\u201d curve as a demonstration that a real dielectric is more reliable; the curve was produced by the stipulated equations. Where the unit involves event probability or a failure model, compute exposure and population definitions before interpreting the percentage.\n\n## Reading order\n\n- Lesson 1.1: **Stress categories and event time scales** \u2014 A protective device located at one bus cannot be assumed to protect a remote device identically during an early front.\n- Lesson 1.2: **Surge impedance and boundary reflections** \u2014 An apparent discrepancy between 60 kV predicted on the right and a measured larger later peak can be due to a secondary reflection, not a basic arithmetic error.\n- Lesson 1.3: **Temporary overvoltage and resonance** \u2014 A planner should distinguish a short-duration peak from a sustained operating-frequency voltage when selecting apparatus duty.\n- Lesson 1.4: **Coordination margins and withstand evidence** \u2014 Before endorsing an insulation level, record the actual equipment-specific rated withstand specification and full simulation envelope.\n\n## Expected outputs\n\nSubmit a two-page technical note, the three-case numerical table, your answer to each lesson's three problems, and one diagram identifying missing evidence. The project and the unit assessment are separate; the project tests reasoning about a whole subsystem, while the assessment checks multiple independent concepts. A valid submission can conclude that physical validation is currently unavailable, provided the logical and numerical work is reproducible.\n\n**Safety boundary.** This is entirely a software and analytical teaching resource. No live high-voltage test or energized hardware construction is included. Lightning, insulation switching and stored electrical energy can cause fatal electric shock or arc events even after isolation. Physical testing requires properly trained personnel, site-specific risk assessments, approved procedures, interlocked laboratories and calibrated equipment; numerical examples provide no permission to undertake those tests.\n"
