          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Lightning Phenomenon: Shielding Failure, Back-Flashover, and Overhead Protection",
          "content": "# Unit 2: Lightning performance of overhead lines\n\n## Engineering overview\n\nA lightning flash affects overhead equipment through multiple paths: direct conductor strikes, shield-wire interceptions, induced surges, tower potential rise and backflashover. Lightning occurrence, strike attachment and insulation performance are stochastic. A power-frequency short-circuit equation is not a lightning attachment model.\n\nFour separate studies are developed in this unit: **Cloud-to-ground stroke and attachment uncertainty**, **Tower potential rise and footing behavior**, **Shielding failure versus backflashover**, and **Outage rate interpretation and exposure**. Their models have intentionally different input assumptions. They should not be combined simply because their numerical outputs share units. A source voltage, local electric field, risk probability and event count answer different questions and can have different uncertainty structures.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of the unit, a reader should be able to state the physical source of the phenomenon; calculate a constrained model result with consistent units; identify its boundary conditions and initial data; conduct a controlled sensitivity test; and construct a short engineering review that explicitly identifies omitted mechanisms. The reader should also be able to separate a documented equipment rating from a quantity invented for an instructional example. A defensible result includes both a number and a statement of its limitations.\n\n## Evidence workflow\n\n1. **Source:** identify what event or operating condition creates the stress or observation. Write down its time reference and appropriate signal representation.\n2. **Propagation or internal response:** trace the signal through the relevant network, insulating material or sensor. Use the model only where its assumptions apply.\n3. **Measurement:** name the quantities that would have to be observed for a real analysis, noting uncertainty, calibration and reference location.\n4. **Decision:** state an actionable conclusion only when the acceptance criterion, equipment definition and hazard boundary are independently documented.\n\n## Integrated software case\n\nPerform a sensitivity study of assumed lightning current and tower impedance. State that the voltage product is a diagnostic idealization and cannot yield a field outage rate without stroke statistics, geometry, grounding and insulation withstand data. Start with the default inputs and write a small table of three cases: baseline, one input increased, and a contrasting configuration. Give units for every column. Do not treat a \u201cbetter-looking\u201d curve as a demonstration that a real dielectric is more reliable; the curve was produced by the stipulated equations. Where the unit involves event probability or a failure model, compute exposure and population definitions before interpreting the percentage.\n\n## Reading order\n\n- Lesson 2.1: **Cloud-to-ground stroke and attachment uncertainty** \u2014 A reliability prediction must separate how often lightning occurs from how it couples to the insulation system.\n- Lesson 2.2: **Tower potential rise and footing behavior** \u2014 Do not use this voltage product as an acceptance criterion for line-string withstand or an energized grounding arrangement.\n- Lesson 2.3: **Shielding failure versus backflashover** \u2014 Line modifications that reduce one failure mode can leave the other unchanged, so mitigation must match the failure mechanism.\n- Lesson 2.4: **Outage rate interpretation and exposure** \u2014 A value of 0.20/year describes expected incidence, not a five-year guarantee or a uniform calendar schedule.\n\n## Expected outputs\n\nSubmit a two-page technical note, the three-case numerical table, your answer to each lesson's three problems, and one diagram identifying missing evidence. The project and the unit assessment are separate; the project tests reasoning about a whole subsystem, while the assessment checks multiple independent concepts. A valid submission can conclude that physical validation is currently unavailable, provided the logical and numerical work is reproducible.\n\n**Safety boundary.** This is entirely a software and analytical teaching resource. No live high-voltage test or energized hardware construction is included. Lightning, insulation switching and stored electrical energy can cause fatal electric shock or arc events even after isolation. Physical testing requires properly trained personnel, site-specific risk assessments, approved procedures, interlocked laboratories and calibrated equipment; numerical examples provide no permission to undertake those tests.\n"
