          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Insulation Coordination: Deterministic and Statistical Approaches, BIL Selection",
          "content": "# Unit 4: Surge arresters and protective architecture\n\n## Engineering overview\n\nGapless metal-oxide arresters provide strongly nonlinear voltage-current behavior, but their protective performance is not described by a single fixed clamp voltage. Terminal inductance, surge shape, energy duty, thermal stability and coordination with neighboring insulation all matter.\n\nFour separate studies are developed in this unit: **Metal-oxide nonlinear characteristic**, **Lead inductance and spatial protection**, **Discharge energy and thermal accumulation**, and **Coordination of protection with equipment**. Their models have intentionally different input assumptions. They should not be combined simply because their numerical outputs share units. A source voltage, local electric field, risk probability and event count answer different questions and can have different uncertainty structures.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of the unit, a reader should be able to state the physical source of the phenomenon; calculate a constrained model result with consistent units; identify its boundary conditions and initial data; conduct a controlled sensitivity test; and construct a short engineering review that explicitly identifies omitted mechanisms. The reader should also be able to separate a documented equipment rating from a quantity invented for an instructional example. A defensible result includes both a number and a statement of its limitations.\n\n## Evidence workflow\n\n1. **Source:** identify what event or operating condition creates the stress or observation. Write down its time reference and appropriate signal representation.\n2. **Propagation or internal response:** trace the signal through the relevant network, insulating material or sensor. Use the model only where its assumptions apply.\n3. **Measurement:** name the quantities that would have to be observed for a real analysis, noting uncertainty, calibration and reference location.\n4. **Decision:** state an actionable conclusion only when the acceptance criterion, equipment definition and hazard boundary are independently documented.\n\n## Integrated software case\n\nCompare two hypothetical nonlinear arrester characteristics in software and explain why a catalogue residual-voltage curve cannot be replaced by a fitted classroom power law. Start with the default inputs and write a small table of three cases: baseline, one input increased, and a contrasting configuration. Give units for every column. Do not treat a \u201cbetter-looking\u201d curve as a demonstration that a real dielectric is more reliable; the curve was produced by the stipulated equations. Where the unit involves event probability or a failure model, compute exposure and population definitions before interpreting the percentage.\n\n## Reading order\n\n- Lesson 4.1: **Metal-oxide nonlinear characteristic** \u2014 An arrester that looks effective on a log-current plot can still be exposed to unacceptable thermal duty.\n- Lesson 4.2: **Lead inductance and spatial protection** \u2014 The geometry of the connection matters to fast-front performance; a single catalogue number is insufficient.\n- Lesson 4.3: **Discharge energy and thermal accumulation** \u2014 Two impulses with identical peaks can have substantially different absorbed energy and thermal impact.\n- Lesson 4.4: **Coordination of protection with equipment** \u2014 A risk register should mark any incomparable test and simulation quantities as an evidence gap rather than invent a margin.\n\n## Expected outputs\n\nSubmit a two-page technical note, the three-case numerical table, your answer to each lesson's three problems, and one diagram identifying missing evidence. The project and the unit assessment are separate; the project tests reasoning about a whole subsystem, while the assessment checks multiple independent concepts. A valid submission can conclude that physical validation is currently unavailable, provided the logical and numerical work is reproducible.\n\n**Safety boundary.** This is entirely a software and analytical teaching resource. No live high-voltage test or energized hardware construction is included. Lightning, insulation switching and stored electrical energy can cause fatal electric shock or arc events even after isolation. Physical testing requires properly trained personnel, site-specific risk assessments, approved procedures, interlocked laboratories and calibrated equipment; numerical examples provide no permission to undertake those tests.\n"
