          "content": "# Unit 5: HVDC insulation and field redistribution\n\n## Engineering overview\n\nDirect voltage stresses solid and composite dielectrics differently from alternating voltage. Under sustained DC the field can depend on conductivity and temperature, while initial charge placement depends strongly on permittivity. Space charge and polarity reversals invalidate many textbook steady-state approximations.\n\nFour separate studies are developed in this unit: **Permittivity-controlled versus conductivity-controlled field**, **Dielectric relaxation and charging transient**, **Polarity reversal and stored interface charge**, and **Thermoelectric coupling and ageing duty**. Their models have intentionally different input assumptions. They should not be combined simply because their numerical outputs share units. A source voltage, local electric field, risk probability and event count answer different questions and can have different uncertainty structures.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of the unit, a reader should be able to state the physical source of the phenomenon; calculate a constrained model result with consistent units; identify its boundary conditions and initial data; conduct a controlled sensitivity test; and construct a short engineering review that explicitly identifies omitted mechanisms. The reader should also be able to separate a documented equipment rating from a quantity invented for an instructional example. A defensible result includes both a number and a statement of its limitations.\n\n## Evidence workflow\n\n1. **Source:** identify what event or operating condition creates the stress or observation. Write down its time reference and appropriate signal representation.\n2. **Propagation or internal response:** trace the signal through the relevant network, insulating material or sensor. Use the model only where its assumptions apply.\n3. **Measurement:** name the quantities that would have to be observed for a real analysis, noting uncertainty, calibration and reference location.\n4. **Decision:** state an actionable conclusion only when the acceptance criterion, equipment definition and hazard boundary are independently documented.\n\n## Integrated software case\n\nSimulate a layered ideal planar DC insulation system. Investigate the change in field partition as conductivity contrast varies and document why temperature- and field-dependent conductivity would change conclusions. Start with the default inputs and write a small table of three cases: baseline, one input increased, and a contrasting configuration. Give units for every column. Do not treat a \u201cbetter-looking\u201d curve as a demonstration that a real dielectric is more reliable; the curve was produced by the stipulated equations. Where the unit involves event probability or a failure model, compute exposure and population definitions before interpreting the percentage.\n\n## Reading order\n\n- Lesson 5.1: **Permittivity-controlled versus conductivity-controlled field** \u2014 No insulation suitability can be concluded from either a short-time dielectric model or one long-time field partition.\n- Lesson 5.2: **Dielectric relaxation and charging transient** \u2014 A model started directly in its DC steady state may underestimate a polarity-reversal transient.\n- Lesson 5.3: **Polarity reversal and stored interface charge** \u2014 A polarity-reversal study needs measured or modelled initial charge distribution and transient limits, not just initial terminal voltage.\n- Lesson 5.4: **Thermoelectric coupling and ageing duty** \u2014 A designer must distinguish a lumped thermal demonstration from a qualified cable ampacity calculation.\n\n## Expected outputs\n\nSubmit a two-page technical note, the three-case numerical table, your answer to each lesson's three problems, and one diagram identifying missing evidence. The project and the unit assessment are separate; the project tests reasoning about a whole subsystem, while the assessment checks multiple independent concepts. A valid submission can conclude that physical validation is currently unavailable, provided the logical and numerical work is reproducible.\n\n**Safety boundary.** This is entirely a software and analytical teaching resource. No live high-voltage test or energized hardware construction is included. Lightning, insulation switching and stored electrical energy can cause fatal electric shock or arc events even after isolation. Physical testing requires properly trained personnel, site-specific risk assessments, approved procedures, interlocked laboratories and calibrated equipment; numerical examples provide no permission to undertake those tests.\n"
