          "heading": "Engineering Design Project & Numerical Investigation: Surge Protection: Spark Gaps, Modern Metal-Oxide (ZnO) Surge Arresters",
          "content": "# Unit 5 investigation \u2014 HVDC insulation and field redistribution\n\n## Design brief\n\nA hypothetical network operator requests a desk-based technical comparison of two options affecting **hvdc insulation and field redistribution**. Both options are abstractions; no physical components, voltages or laboratory procedures are supplied. You may use the teaching equations and the interactive model, but you must not turn synthetic assumptions into claimed equipment specifications.\n\nDirect voltage stresses solid and composite dielectrics differently from alternating voltage. Under sustained DC the field can depend on conductivity and temperature, while initial charge placement depends strongly on permittivity. Space charge and polarity reversals invalidate many textbook steady-state approximations.\n\n## Required deliverables\n\n**A. Analytical framework.** Draft a block diagram connecting the initiating phenomenon, medium or circuit, local stress, sensor evidence and possible decision. Include at least two feedback or hidden variables. For the chosen operating condition, describe why permittivity-controlled versus conductivity-controlled field and polarity reversal and stored interface charge may have competing impacts. Demonstrate with one of the unit's equations, with dimensional units shown.\n\n**B. Three-case calculation.** Simulate a layered ideal planar DC insulation system. Investigate the change in field partition as conductivity contrast varies and document why temperature- and field-dependent conductivity would change conclusions. Make the baseline and both variations reproducible. Label input values as invented, externally sourced, or assumptions. Where the change is nonlinear, explain the physical intuition and derive the relevant numerical scaling rather than claiming every input behaves linearly.\n\n**C. Sensitivity and model breakdown.** Consider specifically: Real composite insulation has many relaxation times and space-charge transport that cannot be summarized by one bulk \u03c4. and Actual cable thermal behavior is distributed, ambient-dependent and coupled nonlinearly to conduction and dielectric loss. These are different failure mechanisms; decide which one would most affect the proposed comparative result. Record at least one missing distribution, geometry, time profile, material constant or calibration measurement.\n\n**D. Verification plan.** Propose a staged desk review: compare limit cases, check units, reproduce one point independently, vary numerical timestep if appropriate, compare an independent data source, and request qualified engineering review before any real installation recommendation. No energized experiment or switchgear operation is permitted as part of this project.\n\n**E. Decision memorandum.** State a conservative conditional conclusion: what the toy model predicts, what remains unknown and which documented evidence could change the decision. Include at least two alternatives and explain why choosing the most attractive plot alone is insufficient.\n\n## Rubric\n\nModel correctness 25%, units and reproduction 20%, sensitivity reasoning 20%, evidence and uncertainty 20%, decision clarity 15%. A polished figure without a correct scope declaration earns no credit for validation.\n\n**Safety boundary.** This is entirely a software and analytical teaching resource. No live high-voltage test or energized hardware construction is included. Lightning, insulation switching and stored electrical energy can cause fatal electric shock or arc events even after isolation. Physical testing requires properly trained personnel, site-specific risk assessments, approved procedures, interlocked laboratories and calibrated equipment; numerical examples provide no permission to undertake those tests.\n"
