          "content": "# Unit 8 investigation \u2014 Reliability and engineering decisions\n\n## Design brief\n\nA hypothetical network operator requests a desk-based technical comparison of two options affecting **reliability and engineering decisions**. Both options are abstractions; no physical components, voltages or laboratory procedures are supplied. You may use the teaching equations and the interactive model, but you must not turn synthetic assumptions into claimed equipment specifications.\n\nAsset management integrates stress, condition, failure consequence, uncertainty and maintenance logistics. A low calculated event probability is not a guarantee and a high alarm score is not automatically a reason for immediate replacement. System engineers justify decisions through defined evidence and responsibility.\n\n## Required deliverables\n\n**A. Analytical framework.** Draft a block diagram connecting the initiating phenomenon, medium or circuit, local stress, sensor evidence and possible decision. Include at least two feedback or hidden variables. For the chosen operating condition, describe why failure hazard and survival models and maintenance availability and outages may have competing impacts. Demonstrate with one of the unit's equations, with dimensional units shown.\n\n**B. Three-case calculation.** Build a basic time-to-event model and a decision register, explicitly including detection delays, external dependencies and qualified review before operational change. Make the baseline and both variations reproducible. Label input values as invented, externally sourced, or assumptions. Where the change is nonlinear, explain the physical intuition and derive the relevant numerical scaling rather than claiming every input behaves linearly.\n\n**C. Sensitivity and model breakdown.** Consider specifically: Consequence distributions may have heavy tails; safety-critical harm cannot be justified away by a small mean cost. and A financial scalar cannot alone decide among options involving injury hazards, severe public impact or noncompliance. These are different failure mechanisms; decide which one would most affect the proposed comparative result. Record at least one missing distribution, geometry, time profile, material constant or calibration measurement.\n\n**D. Verification plan.** Propose a staged desk review: compare limit cases, check units, reproduce one point independently, vary numerical timestep if appropriate, compare an independent data source, and request qualified engineering review before any real installation recommendation. No energized experiment or switchgear operation is permitted as part of this project.\n\n**E. Decision memorandum.** State a conservative conditional conclusion: what the toy model predicts, what remains unknown and which documented evidence could change the decision. Include at least two alternatives and explain why choosing the most attractive plot alone is insufficient.\n\n## Rubric\n\nModel correctness 25%, units and reproduction 20%, sensitivity reasoning 20%, evidence and uncertainty 20%, decision clarity 15%. A polished figure without a correct scope declaration earns no credit for validation.\n\n**Safety boundary.** This is entirely a software and analytical teaching resource. No live high-voltage test or energized hardware construction is included. Lightning, insulation switching and stored electrical energy can cause fatal electric shock or arc events even after isolation. Physical testing requires properly trained personnel, site-specific risk assessments, approved procedures, interlocked laboratories and calibrated equipment; numerical examples provide no permission to undertake those tests.\n"
